WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.525
Client-side encrypted DNS to a public
resolver is the most common bypass.

2
00:00:05.525 --> 00:00:12.180
M-22-09 wants encrypted DNS wherever
supported and endpoints set explicitly to

3
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:17.019
agency resolvers; CISA's target
state has Protective DNS upstream

4
00:00:17.019 --> 00:00:21.600
and endpoints unable to reach
third-party DNS over any protocol.

5
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.712
So encrypted DNS must terminate at an
agency resolver or Protective DNS.

6
00:00:27.712 --> 00:00:30.062
Each transport leaves a different handle.

7
00:00:30.062 --> 00:00:35.662
Plain DNS on 53 can be intercepted,
redirected and blocked by port.

8
00:00:35.662 --> 00:00:42.070
DNS over TLS and DNS over QUIC, on port
853, can't be redirected without breaking

9
00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:48.376
the session, but are straightforward
to block except to approved resolvers.

10
00:00:48.376 --> 00:00:54.579
DNS over HTTPS shares 443 with all web
traffic, so redirect-dns can't see it;

11
00:00:54.579 --> 00:01:00.265
NIST suggests RPZ entries for known
DoH names plus firewall rules,

12
00:01:00.265 --> 00:01:02.850
and CISA adds TLS inspection.

13
00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:06.550
An edge-only design can't
govern encrypted DNS.

14
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:11.281
On Windows, the client uses DoH only
with servers on its known list,

15
00:01:11.281 --> 00:01:16.013
which ships with Cloudflare, Google
and Quad9; add the agency's own.

16
00:01:16.013 --> 00:01:21.584
Since June 2026 the Windows Server
2025 DNS Server accepts client DoH,

17
00:01:21.584 --> 00:01:25.138
but its upstream traffic
stays unencrypted.

18
00:01:25.138 --> 00:01:30.385
CISA says IPsec isn't an acceptable
substitute, suggests an encrypting proxy,

19
00:01:30.385 --> 00:01:34.260
requires root hints disabled,
and allows public fallback

20
00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:38.700
resolvers only when Protective
DNS is unavailable, with alerting.

21
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.678
Zero Trust DNS, on Windows
11 Enterprise and Education,

22
00:01:42.678 --> 00:01:48.688
permits outbound traffic only to addresses
its Protective DNS servers resolved.

23
00:01:48.688 --> 00:01:51.113
Browsers resolve names themselves.

24
00:01:51.113 --> 00:01:56.879
The program adopts CISA's configuration:
the OS resolver, or DoH only to agency

25
00:01:56.879 --> 00:02:02.912
resolvers, as mandatory policy, and an
NXDOMAIN answer for Firefox's canary domain.

26
00:02:02.912 --> 00:02:07.357
Microsoft documents that on a
managed device with the policy unset,

27
00:02:07.357 --> 00:02:09.100
Edge sends no DoH queries.
